      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "RDB files can be copied safely while Redis is running",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [8] confirms that Redis is data backup friendly and allows copying RDB files while the database is running.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 8,
          "quote": "Redis is very data backup friendly since you can copy RDB files while the database is running"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "because a new snapshot is written under a temporary name and atomically renamed with rename(2) once it is complete",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [8] explains the mechanism involving a temporary name and atomic renaming using rename(2).",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 8,
          "quote": "while it gets produced it uses a temporary name and is renamed into its final destination atomically using rename(2) only when the new snapshot is complete."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "The rdbcompression option is on by default and compresses string objects with LZF",
      "verdict": "UNSUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The provided passages do not mention `rdbcompression`, LZF, or compression settings.",
      "citations": []
    }
  ]
}

{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim_text": "In March 2026 a SQLite developer found and fixed a rare corruption bug known as the WAL-reset bug.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [0] states that on 2026-03-03, a developer found and fixed the 'WAL-reset bug' which could lead to database corruption in rare cases.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "On 2026-03-03, one of the SQLite developers (Dan) found and fixed a bug that could, in rare cases, lead to database corruption. We call this the \"WAL-reset bug\"."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "It is likely present in every release from 3.7.0 through 3.51.2 and is fixed in 3.51.3 and later, with backports available for 3.44.6 and 3.50.6.",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage states backports are available for 3.44.6 and 3.50.7, not 3.50.6 as claimed in the answer.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 1,
          "quote": "Backports of the fix are available for some earlier releases: 3.44.6 and 3.50.7."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "The bug can only occur when two or more connections in separate threads or processes try to write or checkpoint at the same instant, and it affects rollback-journal databases as well as WAL databases.",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [2] explicitly states the bug 'only affects databases in WAL mode', contradicting the claim that it affects rollback-journal databases.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 2,
          "quote": "The bug only affects databases in WAL mode when there are two or more database connections open on the same file, in separate threads or processes, and when those two connections attempt to write or checkpoint at the same instant."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "The developers reproduced it organically in the lab before writing the fix, and it was assigned a CVE identifier.",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "Passages [3] and [8] state that developers were 'unable to reproduce the bug organically' and had to use special testing logic/code hacks. There is no mention of a CVE identifier in the passages.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 3,
          "quote": "The developers were unable to reproduce the bug organically and had to add special testing logic to SQLite that deliberately triggers the circumstances of the the bug in order to verify that the issue has been fixed."
        },
        {
          "index": 8,
          "quote": "So much so that the SQLite developers were unable to reproduce the bug organically in the lab."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Phil Eaton later published a reproducer that does not need the special testing code hack.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [9] confirms that Phil Eaton devised a reproducer that does not involve using the special testing code hack.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 9,
          "quote": "UPDATE: 2026-08-24 → Phil Eaton has devised a reproducer that does not involve using the special testing code hack."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Because the consequences are serious, the SQLite developers describe upgrading as an emergency.",
