      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 10,
          "quote": "However, this is not an emergency."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim_text": "To switch a running server from RDB to AOF, enable AOF on the live server with CONFIG SET appendonly yes and also update redis.conf, otherwise the change is lost when the server restarts.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [1] explicitly states to enable AOF via `redis-cli config set appendonly yes` and warns that if you forget to update `redis.conf`, changes will be lost upon restart.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 1,
          "quote": "* Enable AOF: `redis-cli config set appendonly yes` ... **IMPORTANT:** Update your `redis.conf` (potentially through `CONFIG REWRITE`) and ensure that it matches the configuration above. If you forget this step, when you restart the server, the configuration changes will be lost"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Redis automatically blocks incoming writes while it converts the dataset.",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [0] states that you enable AOF via CONFIG command on the 'live server', implying it remains operational. Passage [3] mentions stopping writes manually ('Stop all the writes against the database!') as a specific step in one method, but does not state Redis does this automatically during conversion. In fact, standard Redis behavior allows writes during AOF enabling/rewriting.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "enable AOF via CONFIG command on the live server first"
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "quote": "Stop all the writes against the database!"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Before restarting, wait until aof_rewrite_in_progress and aof_rewrite_scheduled are both 0.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [2] explicitly instructs to wait for these values to be 0 before restarting.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 2,
          "quote": "Before restarting the server, wait for AOF rewrite to finish persisting the data. You can do that by watching `INFO persistence`, waiting for `aof_rewrite_in_progress` and `aof_rewrite_scheduled` to be `0`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "On Redis 7.0 or later, an AOF-only instance can be backed up by copying the files in the directory set by the appendfilename option",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [4] states that the directory is determined by the `appenddirname` configuration, not `appendfilename`.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 4,
          "quote": "AOF files are split into multiple files which reside in a single directory determined by the `appenddirname` configuration."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "and copying them while a rewrite is running is safe.",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [4] explicitly states that if copying is done during a rewrite, 'you might end up with an invalid backup' and provides workarounds to disable rewrites.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 4,
          "quote": "However, if this is done during a [rewrite](#log-rewriting), you might end up with an invalid backup."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "To be extra careful you can set auto-aof-rewrite-percentage to 0, which turns off automatic rewrites.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [5] lists turning off automatic rewrites with `CONFIG SET auto-aof-rewrite-percentage 0` as step 1 of the workaround.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 5,
          "quote": "1. Turn off automatic rewrites with<br/> `CONFIG SET` `auto-aof-rewrite-percentage 0`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "If you take hard links to those files, you can re-enable rewrites before the copy finishes, because Redis only appends to files in that directory or completely replaces them.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [6] explains that creating hard links allows re-enabling rewrites after link creation (before copy finishes) because Redis guarantees it only appends or completely replaces files.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 6,
          "quote": "If you want to minimize the time AOF rewrites are disabled you may create hard links to the files in `appenddirname` (in step 3 above) and then re-enable rewrites (step 4) after the hard links are created. Now you can copy/tar the hardlinks... This works because Redis guarantees that it only appends to files in this directory, or completely replaces them if necessary"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "On Redis 6.0, backing up the AOF is as simple as copying the aof file, and Redis can still load the copy even if it lacks the final part.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [7] states that prior to version 7.0.0 (which includes 6.0), backing up is done by copying the file, and Redis can load it even if truncated.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 7,
          "quote": "Prior to version 7.0.0 backing up the AOF file can be done simply by copying the aof file... The file may lack the final part but Redis will still be able to load it"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim_text": "With the default policy of fsync every second, Redis can lose about one second of writes in a disaster.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage explicitly states that with the default policy of fsync every second, you can only lose one second worth of writes.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 1,
          "quote": "With the default policy of fsync every second, write performance is still great. ... so you can only lose one second worth of writes."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "The fsync itself is done on a background thread.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage states that fsync is performed using a background thread.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 1,
          "quote": "fsync is performed using a background thread"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Everysec has been the default since Redis 2.4.",
      "verdict": "UNSUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "While Passage 5 mentions that 'everysec' is likely as fast as snapshotting since version 2.4, and Passage 6 states it is the suggested/default policy, neither passage explicitly states that it has been the default *since* version 2.4.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "With appendfsync always, Redis still performs a separate fsync for every client write even when many writes arrive in parallel.",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "The passages state that with the 'always' policy, if there are multiple parallel writes, Redis tries to perform a single fsync operation (group commit), contradicting the claim that it performs a separate fsync for every client write.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 6,
          "quote": "The `always` policy is very slow in practice, but it supports group commit, so if there are multiple parallel writes Redis will try to perform a single `fsync` operation."
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "quote": "Note that the commands are appended to the AOF after a batch of commands from multiple clients or a pipeline are executed, so it means a single write and a single fsync"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "RDB snapshots, by contrast, are usually taken every five minutes or more.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage 0 states that you'll usually create an RDB snapshot every five minutes or more.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "However you'll usually create an RDB snapshot every five minutes or more"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "If you can live with a few minutes of data loss the docs say RDB alone is fine.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage 3 explicitly states that if you can live with a few minutes of data loss, you can simply use RDB alone.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 3,
          "quote": "If you care a lot about your data, but still can live with a few minutes of data loss in case of disasters, you can simply use RDB alone."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "With fsync disabled, AOF should be exactly as fast as RDB even under high load.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage 2 states that with fsync disabled, AOF should be exactly as fast as RDB even under high load.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 2,
          "quote": "with fsync disabled it should be exactly as fast as RDB even under high load."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
